WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.002 --> 00:00:02.502
(light music)

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.140
- [Marielena] Yellowstone is epic,

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.053
strange, and iconic.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.573
It is well-deserving of
its protected status.

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:17.160
But how did it come to be the worlds

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.647
first National Park?

00:00:18.647 --> 00:00:21.147
(light music)

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:23.950
Archeologists have found evidence of human

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:25.730
activity in Yellowstone that dates back

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.910
at least 11,000 years.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:30.910
Oral histories of Salish Native Americans

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:32.410
suggest their ancestors were here

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.363
3,000 years ago.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.030
Today there are still 26
Native American tribes

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.633
that are connected to this land.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.300
Some of the first
European visitors included

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.303
fur traders and trackers
in the late 1700s.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.910
But the first big incentive for settlers

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:54.498
came in 1863, gold.

00:00:54.498 --> 00:00:58.080
(water sloshing)

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.670
Prospectors flocked to Yellowstone

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.023
in hopes of finding more.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.100
The Northern Pacific
Railroad Company heard

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.653
of the wonders of Yellowstone.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.830
A big attraction like this
could help their plans

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.513
to expand their railroad west.

00:01:13.565 --> 00:01:15.900
So they sponsored the
Washburn-Langford-Doane

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.570
Expedition of 1870.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.690
As the first formal
expedition of Yellowstone,

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.713
they explored vast regions of the park.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:28.690
Including Tower Fall, Yellowstone Lake,

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:30.083
and the geyser basins.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:32.740
Their most memorable achievement,

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.386
naming Old Faithful.

00:01:34.386 --> 00:01:36.800
(light music)

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:39.380
Painter Thomas Moran as
well as a photographer

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:40.810
and sketch artist were also

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:42.033
on the expedition team.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.900
Their work introduced
Yellowstone to the world.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.443
And captured the imagination of Congress.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.130
Then, on March 1st, 1872,

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.150
President Ulysses S. Grant signed an act

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.920
establishing Yellowstone National Park.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.372
The country's very first National Park.

00:02:01.372 --> 00:02:03.872
(light music)

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.163
The Park is around two million acres.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.940
An expansive wilderness with places

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.863
that even today few have seen.

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:16.000
Filled with wildlife including

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.520
285 species of birds.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:21.543
And over 65 species of mammals.

00:02:21.543 --> 00:02:24.920
(wolf howling)

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.120
But what's on top of this park is nothing

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.130
compared to the giant reserve of magma

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.253
that lies below.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.253
Thermal power is what
makes Yellowstone tick.

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:39.430
Old Faithful remains true to its name.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.930
And to this day gushes
up thousands of gallons

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:44.263
of hot water every hour or so.

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:46.763
(light music)

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.590
It's one of the most famous natural

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.010
features in Yellowstone.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:52.563
But, it's not the only one.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:57.630
There are over 10,000 thermal

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:58.833
features in Yellowstone.

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:03.373
Including hot springs, mud pots,

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:05.733
and steam vents.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.810
They sit in one giant
caldera of a super volcano.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:14.053
Some 45 miles across at its widest.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:19.080
2.1 million years ago Yellowstone erupted

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:22.113
and covered over 5,000
square miles with ash.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.480
About 6,000 times the volume of material

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.083
ejected from Mount St. Helens in 1980.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.640
It's among the largest volcano eruptions

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:33.743
known to man.

00:03:37.596 --> 00:03:39.600
Yellowstone is still active and another

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.270
eruption is possible.

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:44.090
But it probably won't
happen in the next thousand

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.093
or even 10,000 years.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:49.750
In the meantime,

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:53.140
Yellowstone hosts millions
of guests every year.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.190
There are now 59 National Parks

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:56.750
in the United States.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:58.600
But Yellowstone will always be

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.003
the world's first.

